--- event authd.logon.attempted
tier: essential
gating: decoded logon requests reaching authority handling
cardinality: once per decoded `LogonStart` handled; a connection that opens with anything else writes none

A client requested a logon, and the conversation ended.

The event records the outcome after a decoded logon request completes.
Malformed opening messages and connections that fail before a request is
identified do not produce this event. A successful credential check is
separate evidence; see `lpsd.credential.verified`.

The supplied account name is never recorded. On failure it is unverified
input, and the authority does not distinguish an unknown account from a
wrong credential. On success, `subject.token.sid` identifies the granted
principal. A minted token that could not be delivered produces an
`undelivered` outcome with its session identifier.

This essential event records the authority's logon decision and delivery
outcome. It does not prove that the client installed the token or started
a usable session.

field: source.token.sid                     required
  The originator: the principal of the process on the other end of
  `/run/logon.sock` that asked for the logon.
field: source.address                       optional
  The remote peer the originator says the logon is for, where it named one
  and it is an IP address. It is the originator's word, as `login -h` or
  sshd passes it; a host name is not recorded.
field: object.session.logon-type            required
  The logon type the originator asked for, whether or not it was granted.
field: object.session.auth-package          optional
  The principal source authd asked to verify the credential. Absent when
  the logon was refused before a source was chosen.
field: subject.token.sid                    when outcome.success == true
  The principal signed in: the user security identifier (SID) of the token.
field: object.session.id                    optional
  The logon session created for the sign-on. Every record of what the
  principal does in it carries the same value as `subject.token.auth-id`.
  Present whenever a token was minted: on a success, and on an
  `undelivered` failure.
field: object.token.id                      optional
  The minted token identifier, when the token statistics query succeeds.
  A session identifier can be present without this field.
field: outcome.success                      required
  True only when the token reached the originator.
field: outcome.reason                       when outcome.success == false
  values: malformed-request | unsupported-version | permission-denied | authentication-failed | logon-type-not-permitted | account-restricted | authority-unavailable | conversation-limit | internal | abandoned | undelivered
  closed: false
  The denial authd sent the originator, in kebab-case, whether authd decided
  it or relayed it from the source; `authentication-failed` covers an
  unknown principal and a wrong credential alike. `abandoned` is a
  conversation that ended without a terminal answer, because the
  originator's or the source's connection failed. `undelivered` is a token
  minted that could not be handed over; its session is reaped once nothing
  holds it.

--- event authd.service.attested
tier: standard
gating: none — every decoded service-attestation request is recorded
cardinality: once per `ServiceAttest` authd reads

A client requested a service token.

Only the service manager, process ID (PID) 1 running as SYSTEM, can attest
service identities. Requests from other peers are refused and recorded.
An empty service name is also recorded as a refusal, without
`object.service.name`. See PGSS Logon §2.19.

The event distinguishes token creation from successful delivery. It uses
the standard tier because non-SYSTEM services request tokens on each start.

field: subject.token.sid                    required
  The caller: the user security identifier (SID) of the peer token, SYSTEM for
  the service manager.
field: object.service.name                  optional
  The service the token is for, as the service manager named it. Absent
  only when the request named no service.
field: object.token.sid                     optional
  The identity attested: the service's virtual account, a built-in
  service identity such as LocalService, or a principal designated for
  service logon. Present whenever a token was minted: on a success, and on
  an `undelivered` failure.
field: object.session.id                    optional
  The logon session created for the service, present exactly when
  `object.token.sid` is.
field: object.token.id                      optional
  The minted token identifier, when the token statistics query succeeds.
  It can be absent even when `object.token.sid` is present.
field: object.token.privileges              optional
  The privileges local policy granted the token, all of them enabled.
  Present exactly when `object.token.sid` is.
field: outcome.success                      required
  True only when the token reached the service manager.
field: outcome.reason                       when outcome.success == false
  values: permission-denied | malformed-request | account-restricted | internal | undelivered
  closed: false
  The denial authd sent. `permission-denied` is a peer that is not the
  service manager; `account-restricted` an identity no source holds, or
  one not designated for service logon, which authd does not tell apart;
  `undelivered` a token minted that could not be handed over.

--- event authd.session.ended
tier: essential
gating: none — every session end authd carries out is recorded; a refused request and a query are not
cardinality: once per `SessionEnd` authd acts on

The authority attempted to end a logon session at a client's request.

The operation stops processes whose primary tokens belong to the session.
The result can report remaining processes. Even a successful result does
not prove that every token reference has been released. The kernel records
`kacs.session.destroyed` when the session's last reference is released;
this event records who requested the operation. See PGSS Logon §2.22.

Permission queries and refused requests do not produce this event. The
access check for ending another account's session can produce
`kacs.audit.access.checked` with `object.kind` set to `authd-session-end`,
if the descriptor's system access control list (SACL) requests auditing.

This essential event records a session-management action that can stop
another account's work.

field: subject.token.sid                    required
  The principal that asked: themselves, signing out, or someone the
  descriptor permits to end other principals' sessions.
field: object.session.id                    required
field: object.session.user.sid              required
  Whose session it was.
field: object.session.logon-type            required
field: outcome.success                      required
  True when the final process scan reported no remaining session processes.
  This does not prove that no external token references remain.
field: outcome.reason                       when outcome.success == false
  values: processes-remaining
  closed: false
  Some processes survived every round, or could not be examined.
  Session destruction depends on release of every token reference.
